Subject: [PATCH] EE thread (FPS round 4): constant scratchpad accesses and the DMA drain inline, a leaner
 dispatchGuestCall; call-site, leaf and loop-checkpoint inlining options (off)

The game thread (EE) is the frame-rate limit in levels (TODO 94). Measured first with a new aid,
PS2X_HOT_STATS=1 (every 20 s of wall time: guest calls by target, loop checkpoints, and the loads/stores
that reach PS2Runtime::Load*/Store*, to stderr): in Amazon ~7.5 million calls a second, ~5 million loop
checkpoints and ~5.9 million Load*/Store* calls - all of them to the scratchpad at a constant address.
Each change runs the same guest code in the same order with the same EE clock: the scripted cold boot into
Amazon and the fight route (aim, walk, fire, melee, car), all in turbo with VIF1 on the EE thread, give the
same game state on every game tick as before (8,047 and 19,244 ticks, on the test PC, the other PC and
locally), and the native renderer's shots are pixel-identical.

Kept (on):
- ps2xRecomp config `inline_scratchpad_constants`: a load/store at a constant scratchpad address (aligned,
  inside its 16 KB - what the analysis marks MMIO) becomes PS2_SPR_LOADn(addr) / PS2_SPR_STOREn(addr, v)
  (ps2_runtime_macros.h): the scratchpad's memory read/written in place, as PS2Runtime::Load*/Store* do
  first; a 32-bit store still drains the completed DMA handlers after it, as Store32 does.
  PS2X_SCRATCHPAD_CONST=0: the runtime calls.
- PS2Runtime::drainCompletedDmacHandlersFast (runtime/ps2_leaf_call.h): drainCompletedDmacHandlers' "nothing
  queued" test (the same flag, PS2X_EE_FASTPATHS bit 2) in place, for WRITE32's inline scratchpad way and
  PS2_SPR_STORE32. PS2X_DRAIN_INLINE=0: the call every time.
- dispatchGuestCall leaner: the common end of a call (back at the fallthrough, not stopped, no caller of this
  level to resume) tested first and taken in a few instructions - what afterCall does then; every other end
  through callEnd, now PS2_NOINLINE. The dispatch history recorded on this path only with
  PS2X_DISPATCH_HISTORY=1 (the lookup, branch and continued calls still record it). PS2X_DISPATCH_LEAN=0: as
  before.
- The dispatcher's call levels as a public thread-local (Ps2DispatchLevels g_ps2DispatchLevels, trivially
  constructible so that no access needs the on-demand TLS initialization), shared by enterCall/callBegin/
  callEnd and the inline ways below.

Options, measured slower or even and left off in the port (each with its run-time switch):
- `direct_call_sites`: Ps2CallKnown's begin and end now inline (PS2Runtime::leafBegin/leafEnd) instead of
  callBegin/callEnd. PS2X_DIRECT_CALLS=1: dispatchGuestCall.
- `indirect_call_sites`: a jalr made at its site (Ps2CallIndirect: the target's table entry read in place,
  between leafBegin and leafEnd); no entry or PS2X_INDIRECT_CALLS=0: dispatchGuestBranch.
- `inline_loop_checkpoints`: a loop's checkpoint through PS2Runtime::eeLoopCheckpoint (the scheduler's
  checkpointDue(32) inline). PS2X_INLINE_CHECKPOINT=0: eeCheckpointDue.
- `inline_leaves = N`: a jal to a leaf function (no call, no jump out, no backward goto, no syscall/break/
  trap, no switch, no runtime call but the stores', nothing thrown, ends in `jr $ra`) of at most N
  instructions made with the leaf's code at the site: each caller's file gets `leaf_<name>` (the code as a
  static force-inlined function) and `Ps2CallLeaf_<name>` (while the table entry is still <name>:
  leafBegin, the body, leafEnd; else dispatchGuestCall). The recompiler finds the leaves
  (PS2Recompiler::buildInlineLeaves) and the register cache knows the new call forms.
  PS2X_INLINE_LEAVES=0: dispatchGuestCall.
Each of these puts ~50-70 instructions (or the leaf's body) at every site: the generated code grew by
20-50% and the game thread was even or 1-4% slower.

On a GTX 1080 test PC, Amazon from its first gate, the user's settings at 1080 lines, 120 held, game ticks
8600-13000; the other PC at 240: see TODO 94's round-4 paragraph.
